  "implementation": [
    "Own the underlying surface through Arc and retain face orientation. Query the existing closest-parameter and regular-normal kernels to orient target sections, overriding target Roadlike up. A straight automatic source uses world Z with its deterministic vertical-line fallback. General spatial target curves can use surface orientation without the unqualified automatic frame selector.",
    "Query normals at the full axial destination, including finite-guide endpoint residuals, before applying transverse coordinates. Preserve guide positions and independently generated arc-length lookup data. Skip unused transported frame preparation for surface targets.",
    "For unstretched nonlinear NURBS targets longer than the base, independently invert the source-length fraction with incremental tolerance 1e-6, trim a private curve copy, and build the prefix inverse table. Normalize it by source length while retaining original guide geometry and total length. The inferred preparation rule matches the retained public SDK maps; native arrays, cuts, normals and UVs remain expectations only.",
    "Reuse selectable surface/B-rep face resolution and immutable snapshots. Register TargetSurface=id|None and TargetSurfaceFace=parent,index; exclude references from sources and reject their grips. Collect both Roadlike axes as native does; the surface controls target orientation. Reset the surface reference after success.",
    "Release source object and grip selection before recording TargetSurface replacements/copies, retaining native final selection in Undo/Redo. Reuse staged ordinary/grip/group edits. Refine existing geometry bounds numerically in both command and Flow preview for rigid placement centers so Local derivatives do not amplify model-tolerance center error.",
    "Interactive surface and face picks, SelID/Face input, reference changes/removal, live preview and one staged commit share the kernel. Hidden, locked, replaced and deleted references invalidate previews. Cancellation and reference getters do not deform the document."
  ],
